## Testing Private Methods

## The Core Principle

Private methods are implementation details. If you change how a private method works internally — without changing the observable behaviour — tests should still pass. Testing private methods directly couples tests to implementation, making refactoring painful.

## Strategy 1: Test Through the Public API

```typescript
class PriceCalculator {
  calculate(items: CartItem[], userId: string): number {
    const subtotal = this.sumItems(items);      // private
    const discount = this.getDiscount(userId);   // private
    return subtotal * (1 - discount);
  }

  private sumItems(items: CartItem[]): number { ... }
  private getDiscount(userId: string): number { ... }
}

// ✅ Test through calculate() — exercises the private methods indirectly
test('applies 20% discount for premium users', () => {
  const calc = new PriceCalculator();
  const result = calc.calculate([{ price: 100 }], 'premium-user-id');
  expect(result).toBe(80);
});
```

## Strategy 2: Extract to a Separate Class (the Design Signal)

If `getDiscount` is complex enough to test independently, it probably belongs in its own class:

```typescript
// Extract the private logic into a testable unit
class DiscountPolicy {
  getDiscount(userId: string): number { // now public
    // complex logic here
  }
}

// Now you can test it directly
test('returns 0.2 for premium users', () => {
  const policy = new DiscountPolicy();
  expect(policy.getDiscount('premium-user')).toBe(0.2);
});
```

## Strategy 3: Type Assertion Escape Hatch (TypeScript)

```typescript
// ⚠️ Last resort — fragile, but sometimes pragmatic
const calc = new PriceCalculator();
const result = (calc as any).sumItems([{ price: 50 }, { price: 30 }]);
expect(result).toBe(80);
// This will break silently if you rename the method
```

## Strategy 4: Protected + Test Subclass

```typescript
class PriceCalculator {
  protected getDiscount(userId: string): number { ... } // changed to protected
}

// In test file only:
class TestablePriceCalculator extends PriceCalculator {
  public exposedGetDiscount(userId: string) {
    return this.getDiscount(userId);
  }
}
```

## Decision Matrix

| Situation | Action |
|---|---|
| Simple private helper | Test through public API |
| Complex business logic in private method | Extract to a new class |
| Legacy code you can't refactor | `as any` or reflection |
| Inherited private logic | Protected + subclass |

## Summary

Feeling the urge to test a private method is a design smell. Use it as a prompt to refactor rather than reaching for `as any`.

## Testing Database-Driven Applications

## The Core Strategy: Separate Concerns

```
Business Logic Layer  ←  tested with unit tests (mock DB)
Repository Layer      ←  tested with integration tests (real DB)
Database              ←  never hit in unit tests
```

## Step 1: Use the Repository Pattern

```typescript
// Define an interface — your business logic depends on the interface, not the DB
interface UserRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  save(user: User): Promise<User>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}

// Real implementation (used in production)
class SupabaseUserRepository implements UserRepository {
  async findById(id: string) {
    const { data } = await supabase.from('users').select().eq('id', id).single();
    return data;
  }
  // ...
}
```

## Step 2: Unit Test with a Mock Repository

```typescript
// Your business logic (easily testable)
class UserService {
  constructor(private repo: UserRepository) {}

  async deactivateUser(id: string): Promise<void> {
    const user = await this.repo.findById(id);
    if (!user) throw new Error('User not found');
    await this.repo.save({ ...user, active: false });
  }
}

// Unit test — no database
test('deactivateUser throws when user not found', async () => {
  const mockRepo: UserRepository = {
    findById: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue(null),
    save: jest.fn(),
    delete: jest.fn(),
  };
  const service = new UserService(mockRepo);

  await expect(service.deactivateUser('missing-id')).rejects.toThrow('User not found');
  expect(mockRepo.save).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

## Step 3: Integration Test with a Real Test Database

```typescript
// Uses a real Supabase test project / local Supabase
beforeEach(async () => {
  await testDb.from('users').delete().neq('id', ''); // clean slate
  await testDb.from('users').insert(testFixtures.users);
});

test('findById returns the correct user', async () => {
  const repo = new SupabaseUserRepository(testDb);
  const user = await repo.findById('fixture-user-1');
  expect(user?.name).toBe('Aaron');
});
```

