## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

```
❌ Unit tests that hit a real database (slow, flaky, ordering issues)
❌ Sharing a database between tests (test pollution)
❌ Not cleaning up between tests (false positives/negatives)
❌ Mocking the ORM instead of the repository (too low-level)
```

## Summary

| Test Type | Database | What It Tests |
|---|---|---|
| Unit | Mocked repo | Business logic |
| Integration | Real test DB | Repository queries |
| E2E | Real test DB | Full user flow |

## Unit Testing a GUI

## Core Principle: Test Behaviour, Not Structure

```typescript
// ❌ Implementation-coupled (fragile)
const button = container.querySelector('.btn-primary.submit-btn');

// ✅ Behaviour-focused (resilient)
const button = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i });
```

## React Testing Library Approach

```typescript
import { render, screen, waitFor } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import LoginForm from './LoginForm';

test('shows error when login fails', async () => {
  // ARRANGE
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  const mockOnLogin = jest.fn().mockRejectedValue(new Error('Invalid credentials'));
  render(<LoginForm onLogin={mockOnLogin} />);

  // ACT
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'test@example.com');
  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/password/i), 'wrongpassword');
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /sign in/i }));

  // ASSERT
  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toHaveTextContent('Invalid credentials');
});
```

## Query Priority (Testing Library hierarchy)

```
1. getByRole         — most accessible, preferred
2. getByLabelText    — form fields
3. getByPlaceholderText — fallback for inputs
4. getByText        — visible text content
5. getByTestId      — last resort (adds test coupling)
```

## Async UI Updates

```typescript
// Component loads data on mount
test('displays users after fetch', async () => {
  jest.spyOn(api, 'getUsers').mockResolvedValue([{ name: 'Aaron' }]);
  render(<UserList />);

  // findBy* waits for the element to appear
  expect(await screen.findByText('Aaron')).toBeInTheDocument();
});

// waitFor for complex async assertions
test('hides spinner after load', async () => {
  render(<UserList />);
  await waitFor(() => {
    expect(screen.queryByRole('progressbar')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
  });
});
```

## Snapshot Testing

```typescript
// ✅ Good for design system / static components
test('Button renders correctly', () => {
  const { asFragment } = render(<Button variant="primary">Click me</Button>);
  expect(asFragment()).toMatchSnapshot();
});

// ❌ Bad for feature components — snapshots become noise
```

## What Unit Tests Cover vs E2E

| Scenario | Unit (RTL) | E2E (Playwright) |
|---|---|---|
| Form validation | ✅ | |
| API error states | ✅ | |
| Component interactions | ✅ | |
| Full login + redirect flow | | ✅ |
| Payment checkout | | ✅ |

## Adding Tests to a Legacy/Existing Project

## The Wrong Approach

```
❌ "Let's get to 80% coverage in one sprint"
→ Rushed, brittle tests that test nothing real
→ Team demoralised, tests abandoned after 2 months
```

## The Right Approach: Incremental, Targeted

### Phase 1: Stop the Bleeding (Week 1)

```
✅ New code rule: no PR merged without tests for the new logic
✅ Bug rule: write a failing test before fixing every bug
   - Documents the bug
   - Proves the fix works
   - Prevents regression
```

### Phase 2: Target High-Risk Areas

```typescript
// Run coverage to find the gaps
// npx jest --coverage

// Focus on:
// 1. Business-critical logic (payments, auth, data transforms)
// 2. High-churn files (frequently modified = frequently broken)
// 3. Code with a history of bugs
// 4. Shared utilities used everywhere
```

### Phase 3: Break Dependencies Before Testing

Legacy code is often untestable because of tight coupling:

```typescript
// ❌ Before: hardcoded dependency — impossible to test without the DB
class OrderService {
  async placeOrder(items: Item[]) {
    const db = new DatabaseConnection(); // hardcoded!
    await db.insert('orders', { items });
  }
}

// ✅ After: injected dependency — testable
class OrderService {
  constructor(private db: OrderRepository) {}

  async placeOrder(items: Item[]) {
    await this.db.insert({ items }); // now mockable
  }
}
```

### Phase 4: Use the Strangler Fig Pattern

For large legacy modules, don't rewrite — wrap:

```typescript
// New, tested wrapper around old untested code
class ModernPaymentService {
  constructor(private legacyPayment: LegacyPaymentModule) {}

  async charge(amount: number, card: CardDetails): Promise<Receipt> {
    // New validated, tested entry point
    if (amount <= 0) throw new Error('Amount must be positive');
    return this.legacyPayment.processCharge(amount, card);
  }
}
// Test the new wrapper; leave legacy internals alone
```

## Realistic Timeline

```
Month 1: New code + bug-fix tests → coverage crawls up
Month 2-3: Refactor + test high-risk modules
Month 6: Meaningful coverage on critical paths
Month 12: Broad coverage across the codebase
```

## Tooling That Helps

```
jest --coverage --changedSince=main  # only coverage for changed files
jest --watch                          # run on save during development
```
